const cnidTerms=[{word:'Cnidarian',definition:'An animal belonging to Cnidaria, the group containing corals, anemones, hydroids, and jellyfish.'},{word:'Cnidocyte',definition:'A specialized cell containing a dischargeable capsule used in functions such as capturing prey or defense.'},{word:'Nematocyst',definition:'A capsule inside a cnidocyte that can discharge a thread; many types deliver a sting.'},{word:'Polyp',definition:'A body form with a mouth and tentacles usually directed away from an attached base.'},{word:'Medusa',definition:'A bell-shaped body form associated with swimming jellyfish.'}];
const anemTerms=[{word:'Pedal disc',definition:'The attachment surface at the base of many sea anemones.'},{word:'Oral disc',definition:'The upper surface around an anemone’s mouth, bordered by tentacles.'},{word:'Gastrovascular cavity',definition:'The internal digestive space that also distributes materials through the body.'},{word:'Symbiosis',definition:'A close association between different species; its effects depend on the organisms and conditions.'},{word:'Fission',definition:'A form of asexual reproduction in which one animal divides to produce new individuals.'}];
export const cnidarianGuides={
 cnidaria:{title:'Cnidaria: stinging cells, shared structures, different lives',question:'How can a coral, a jellyfish, and an anemone belong to the same animal group?',abstract:'Cnidarians are animals with specialized cells containing dischargeable capsules. Their group includes corals, anemones, hydroids, and jellyfish. A polyp and a medusa are two important body forms, but not every species has both. Comparing feeding, tissues, colonies, and life cycles helps explain their different roles in marine habitats.',advancedAbstract:'Cnidarian diversity connects cellular mechanisms with tissue organization, body form, and ecological interactions. This article follows the chain from dischargeable capsules to prey capture, digestion, movement, and population processes. Comparisons distinguish a cell from its capsule, an individual from a colony, and a life stage from a species. Light, water flow, and partnerships with other organisms modify outcomes. Scientific explanations become stronger when observations, scale, and alternative causes are made explicit.',goals:['Recognize the major examples of Cnidaria.','Distinguish a stinging cell from its capsule.','Compare polyp and medusa forms in a food web.'],advancedGoals:['Connect tissues and cellular mechanisms with body functions.','Interpret colonies and life cycles at appropriate scales.','Design evidence that separates transport, growth, and reproduction.'],terms:cnidTerms,recap:'Compare the shared stinging-cell machinery first, then ask how body form, life stage, and habitat change what the animal does.',check:{question:'Does every cnidarian spend part of its life as a swimming jellyfish?',explanation:'No. Many species have different life-cycle patterns. Sea anemones and corals do not have a swimming medusa stage, even though larvae may disperse.'},application:'Choose two numbered examples below. Identify a shared feature and a different body arrangement, then explain how the difference affects feeding or movement.',sources:['cnidBody','cnidDiversity','cnidJellies','cnidTissues','cnidCorals','cnidStaghorn','cnidHydroid']},
 anemones:{title:'Anemones: animals that live like living crowns',question:'How can an attached soft-bodied animal capture food, move, and respond to its surroundings?',abstract:'Sea anemones are cnidarian animals, not flowers. Their polyp bodies combine an attachment base, a column, a mouth, and tentacles containing stinging cells. They capture animal food; some also live with photosynthetic partners. Different species show different shapes, habitats, and reproductive strategies. A careful comparison separates these differences from temporary changes in expansion and posture.',advancedAbstract:'Sea anemones provide a focused case study of how soft tissues, fluid pressure, sensory responses, and ecological partnerships interact. This article links attachment and tentacle feeding with digestion, gas exchange, energy budgets, reproduction, and competition. It treats symbiosis as an interaction with conditions and costs, rather than a label that guarantees benefit. Observations of size and abundance are evaluated against alternative explanations such as contraction, movement, recruitment, and sampling bias.',goals:['Identify an anemone’s main body parts.','Explain how tentacles and stinging cells capture food.','Compare selected species and ways they reproduce.'],advancedGoals:['Connect fluid-supported structure with feeding and movement.','Evaluate symbiosis and competition using energy pathways.','Distinguish growth from posture changes and population movement.'],terms:anemTerms,recap:'An anemone’s flowerlike outline belongs to an animal. Its soft body, attachment, tentacles, and partnerships work together under changing conditions.',check:{question:'Would a wider anemone in today’s photograph necessarily have grown since yesterday?',explanation:'No. It may be more expanded, photographed at another scale, or responding to different flow. Compare repeated standardized observations before inferring tissue growth.'},application:'Compare two anemones in the gallery. Name one useful structural difference and one feature a picture alone cannot establish about their biology.',sources:['cnidBody','anemAggregating','anemBeadlet','anemPlumose','anemBubble','cnidCorals']},
};
export const cnidarianIntros={
 cnidaria:{sections:[
 {heading:'Meet the animals behind the shapes',paragraphs:[
  'A jellyfish pulses through the water, a coral colony branches from a reef, and an anemone spreads its tentacles over a rock. These different animals belong to the phylum Cnidaria. A phylum is a large grouping of related animals. Cnidarians have no backbone, and they are not plants, even when a colony looks like a bush or an anemone looks like a flower.',
  'This chapter begins in sunlit water, where corals and many anemones are easy examples to study. The group also includes animals in much deeper water and some freshwater species. A group name does not mean that every member lives in the same habitat. The numbered illustrations compare a few marine examples rather than showing all cnidarian diversity.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Look closely at a stinging cell',paragraphs:[
  'A defining feature is a specialized cell called a cnidocyte. Inside it is a capsule that can discharge a thread. A familiar kind of capsule is a nematocyst. The cell and the capsule are different structures, just as a room and a container inside it are different. Many capsules help catch prey or defend the animal.',
  'Discharge can happen very quickly after suitable stimulation. Not every touch leads to the same response, and different capsule types do different jobs. After a capsule has fired, it is not simply rewound and fired again. The animal must replace spent structures. A tentacle carries many cells, so one discharged capsule does not mean the whole animal has lost its defense.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Compare a polyp with a medusa',paragraphs:[
  'A polyp usually has a column-shaped body with an attached base and a mouth surrounded by tentacles. An anemone is a clear example. A medusa has a bell-shaped body, with the mouth and feeding structures below. The familiar swimming jellyfish is a medusa. These words describe forms, not two separate animal phyla.',
  'Many cnidarians change form during their lives, but there is no single life cycle for all of them. Corals and anemones do not have a swimming medusa stage. Their larvae can still travel before settling. Some hydroids form branching colonies of connected polyps, and other species release small medusae. Ask which species and stage you are observing.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Follow food into a soft body',paragraphs:[
  'Tentacles bring captured food toward a mouth. An internal digestive space helps break food down and distribute materials. A nerve net supports coordinated responses without a centralized brain like a fish’s. Jellyfish also have a thick jelly-like layer between their outer and inner tissues. Body shape, tissues, and water movements work together.',
  'Cnidarians are consumers that eat other organisms. Some corals and anemones also host tiny photosynthetic partners. These partners can provide useful food products, but the animal has not become a plant. A partnership changes the animal’s food supply; it does not change its classification.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Connect an individual with a community',paragraphs:[
  'One coral branch may contain many connected polyps. A colony and one polyp are different levels of organization. Some corals build hard skeletons that add structure to a reef, while other cnidarians do not build that kind of framework. Jellies can eat small animals and become food for larger ones. Their roles depend on the species, size, and surroundings.',
  'Use the plate to compare a moon jelly, staghorn coral, a hydroid, and a bubble-tip anemone. Notice which structures are repeated and which are arranged differently. In the next unit we focus on anemones. Later in this chapter, we return to the group through the lion’s mane jellyfish, one of the largest jellyfish.'
 ]},
 ]},
 anemones:{sections:[
 {heading:'A flowerlike animal on a rock',paragraphs:[
  'A sea anemone may look like a flower opening underwater, but it is an animal. It belongs to Cnidaria, the group introduced in the previous unit. Its body is a polyp: a soft column with a mouth on an oral disc and a crown of tentacles. Many anemones attach to a surface using a pedal disc at the base.',
  'Sea anemones live in many marine environments, from tide pools to deep water. The examples in this unit do not all come from one place. A warm-water bubble-tip anemone and a cool-water plumose anemone face different conditions. Comparing them is useful, but it would be misleading to picture every species living together on the same tropical reef.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Use tentacles to bring food to the mouth',paragraphs:[
  'The tentacles contain cnidocytes, the specialized cells discussed in the Cnidaria unit. Capsules inside those cells can discharge threads that help capture or hold prey. Tentacles then move suitable food toward the mouth. Anemones can feed on small animals brought within reach by water movement, although diets and prey sizes differ between species.',
  'Food enters a gastrovascular cavity, an internal digestive space. The mouth also serves as the route through which undigested remains leave. A sea anemone does not have the long one-way gut of a fish. Its body arrangement works for its way of feeding, rather than being an unfinished version of a different animal.'
 ]},
 {heading:'A soft body can change shape',paragraphs:[
  'Muscles act against fluid within the body, helping the animal expand, contract, and bend. An expanded anemone can present a broad tentacle crown, while a contracted one may look like a small smooth lump. These shapes can belong to the same individual at different times. A change in width does not automatically mean the animal has grown or shrunk in tissue mass.',
  'Attached does not always mean permanently fixed. Many anemones can move slowly on their bases, and some species can detach or use more dramatic movements. A secure position helps with feeding, but conditions can change. Water flow, available space, neighbors, and disturbance can influence where an animal is found.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Partners can add another food pathway',paragraphs:[
  'Some anemones contain microscopic photosynthetic partners in their tissues. In suitable light, the partners can produce organic food and share some products with the host. The animal can still capture prey. Other anemones do not depend on the same kind of partnership, so sunlight alone cannot explain the needs of every species.',
  'Certain fishes and other animals also associate with particular anemones. That does not mean every anemone is a suitable home for every clownfish, or that every association has the same effects. A useful explanation names the partners and asks what each receives. The partnership and its surroundings must be considered together.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Make new animals and share a habitat',paragraphs:[
  'Anemones can reproduce sexually, producing young from eggs and sperm. Some also reproduce asexually, for example by dividing or leaving tissue at the base that develops into new individuals. The available methods differ among species. Anemones do not turn into swimming jellyfish medusae, even though larvae may disperse before settling.',
  'Look at the four numbered illustrations before the quiz. The bubble-tip, beadlet, plumose, and aggregating anemones show different crowns and columns. Match each number with its name and description. The plate shows useful features, but it is not drawn to one scale and cannot reveal the entire life history of an animal.'
 ]},
 ]},
};
export const cnidarianDives={
 cnidaria:{title:'Cnidaria: from a dischargeable capsule to a marine ecosystem',deck:'Connect cellular mechanisms, tissue organization, life cycles, and ecological evidence.',abstract:cnidarianGuides.cnidaria.advancedAbstract,terms:[...cnidTerms,{word:'Colony',definition:'An association of connected animal units, often produced through asexual development.'},{word:'Recruitment',definition:'The addition of individuals to a population or to a defined life stage being studied.'}],question:'A bay contains more jellyfish this week. Design observations that distinguish local reproduction, current-driven transport, and changes in sampling effort.',sections:[
 {heading:'Classification follows relationships rather than appearance',sources:['cnidDiversity','cnidBody'],paragraphs:[
  'A coral colony, an anemone, and a jellyfish can look less alike than two unrelated transparent swimmers. Classification therefore needs more than a quick judgment about shape. Cnidaria includes animals with a characteristic cellular apparatus associated with dischargeable capsules. Comparing tissues, development, and biological relationships supplies a stronger basis for grouping them than deciding whether an organism resembles a flower or a bell. A common name is a useful starting label, but it is not a complete anatomical explanation.',
  'The word jelly is especially easy to overextend. Comb jellies belong to Ctenophora, a different phylum, even though their bodies can also appear gelatinous. Some distant groups have similar solutions to floating and feeding in water. Similar appearance can arise without the organisms sharing every structure or being close relatives. When a photograph does not reveal the diagnostic feature, a cautious identification should say what additional evidence is needed instead of treating a silhouette as proof.',
  'A classroom comparison can begin with a feature matrix. Record whether a specimen shows a bell, an attached base, repeated polyps, and a hard framework, while marking features that are not visible. Missing evidence is different from evidence of absence. A tentacle hidden behind a body has not necessarily been lost. The matrix organizes observations, but a reliable classification still depends on biological reference material and the scale at which the features can be examined.'
 ]},
 {heading:'A cell is not the capsule it contains',sources:['cnidBody','cnidJellies'],paragraphs:[
  'The cnidocyte is a specialized cell; a nematocyst is a capsule housed inside such a cell. Confusing these levels makes the mechanism harder to follow. A useful explanation proceeds from a stimulus, through the cell and capsule, to the discharge of a thread and its effect on a target. The thread may penetrate, attach, or contribute to handling depending on its type. A drawing of one familiar stinging capsule should not be treated as a diagram of every cnidarian capsule.',
  'Discharge releases a prepared structure rather than requiring a thread to be assembled during contact. That distinction helps explain how a very rapid response can occur. It also creates a replacement problem: an already discharged capsule is spent. Continued feeding and defense depend on many cells and the production of replacements, not on repeatedly firing one unchanged capsule. An animation that resets a capsule for review is a teaching replay, not a biological recharge cycle.',
  'Separate different response variables in a proposed investigation. The fraction of capsules that discharge, the speed of discharge, and the success of prey capture are different measurements. A higher discharge fraction would not by itself show that more prey were eaten. Contact conditions and prey behavior could alter the outcome. Microscopic observation and feeding records address different stages of the mechanism; linking them requires a design that follows the same sequence rather than comparing unrelated photographs.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Tissue layers support a coordinated body',sources:['cnidTissues','cnidBody'],paragraphs:[
  'Cnidarian bodies contain organized tissues around an internal digestive space. A jelly-like material between outer and inner tissues contributes to the structure, especially conspicuously in many medusae. Thin tissues and contact with surrounding water influence the movement of dissolved substances. These animals do not need to reproduce the entire organ arrangement of a vertebrate in order to live. The useful question is how materials move through this particular body and how the arrangement constrains its size and activity.',
  'A nerve net provides a means of coordinating responses without a centralized brain arranged like a fish’s. Muscles can alter body shape or produce swimming pulses. Describing the animal as simple should not replace a mechanism: a feeding response still requires sensing a relevant condition, transmitting information, and changing movement. A nerve net is not the absence of a nervous system, just as a digestive cavity is not the absence of digestion.',
  'Imagine comparing two gelatinous bodies with equal volume but different shapes. Differences in surface area and tissue thickness could change exchange with water. This is a model of geometry, not a claim that every real jelly follows the same rate law. Temperature, activity, water movement, and tissue composition would also matter. An explanation becomes more useful when it identifies both the proposed structural effect and the conditions under which it expects that effect to be visible.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Polyp and medusa describe arrangements, not a universal sequence',sources:['cnidBody','cnidJellies'],paragraphs:[
  'The polyp arrangement commonly places an attachment surface at one end and a mouth with tentacles at the other. The medusa arrangement commonly places feeding structures beneath a swimming bell. These forms help compare orientation and function, but they do not define a compulsory two-step life cycle. A species can have a prominent stage, a reduced stage, or no medusa stage at all. Corals and anemones provide important examples that prevent a jellyfish life cycle from becoming a rule for the whole phylum.',
  'A life-cycle diagram must distinguish sexual reproduction, asexual multiplication, larval dispersal, and changes in form. A new-looking stage is not necessarily a different species, and several young animals can arise through asexual processes. An observation of one adult form cannot identify every earlier stage without additional evidence. Repeated observations, culture records, and careful identification connect those stages more reliably than a single specimen collected at one moment.',
  'Consider an investigation that counts large medusae but ignores attached stages. It could describe the visible swimmers accurately while missing processes that influence later abundance. Conversely, a survey of attached polyps cannot automatically predict the number of medusae in the bay next month. Survival, release timing, transport, and species identity all matter. Sampling more than one stage expands the explanation, provided the observations actually belong to the same life cycle.'
 ]},
 {heading:'A colony changes what it means to count an animal',sources:['cnidHydroid','cnidCorals'],paragraphs:[
  'A branching hydroid or coral can contain many repeated polyp units. The colony has a visible outline, yet that outline contains connected living components. The hard framework of a stony coral adds another level of structure. A count of branches, a count of polyps, and a measurement of living cover describe different things. They should not be substituted for one another just because all can be written as numbers in the same notebook.',
  'Suppose one survey records ten colonies while another records thousands of polyps. These results need not disagree; the observers may have used different units. A fair comparison first specifies what was counted, how boundaries were recognized, and whether fragments were treated as new colonies. A photograph cropped differently can also change the apparent amount of branching. Define the biological unit and the measurement procedure before drawing a conclusion about change.',
  'Colony architecture can alter the surrounding habitat by creating surfaces and spaces. That structure can influence other organisms, but a branching outline alone does not quantify its ecological contribution. A study could compare occupied spaces, water movement, or animal use around different colony forms. It would also need to consider size and location. The drawing gives a structural hypothesis; repeated field measurements test how that structure functions in an actual community.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Feeding and photosynthetic partnerships are separate pathways',sources:['cnidCorals','cnidStaghorn'],paragraphs:[
  'Cnidarians are animals that consume other organisms or organic material. Some also host microscopic photosynthetic partners. Products from those partners can contribute to the animal’s energy supply, while the host provides a living environment and exchanged materials. The partnership does not turn the animal’s own cells into a plant. It adds another pathway through which energy and matter reach the host, and the importance of that pathway varies among species and conditions.',
  'To compare energy pathways, distinguish gross production, transfer to a partner, consumption, and use in metabolism. A bright habitat does not guarantee that all captured light becomes animal growth. Food availability, respiration, temperature, and the state of the partnership can alter the balance. An animal may also obtain nutrients by feeding that are not supplied in sufficient amounts by the partnership. A statement that sunlight feeds the reef is a broad summary of several linked processes.',
  'Changes in a partnership can affect appearance and performance. Bleaching in reef-building corals involves disruption or loss of their symbiotic relationship and can expose a pale skeleton through tissue. Pale does not automatically mean already dead, and recovery is not guaranteed. A responsible interpretation distinguishes color change from survival, then asks about the duration and conditions of stress. Following the same colonies through time can supply evidence that a one-day color survey cannot.'
 ]},
 {heading:'A visible aggregation can have several causes',sources:['cnidJellies'],paragraphs:[
  'More jellyfish observed near a shore can result from local population processes, movement, or observation methods. Currents can concentrate animals without producing new ones. A longer survey can find more individuals without a change in density. Clearer water can improve detection. These explanations operate alongside growth and reproduction, so an abundance claim should specify area, duration, depth, and the method used to detect the animals.',
  'An investigation could combine repeated counts with current measurements, size distributions, and observations of relevant life stages. A concentration that follows a water-movement pattern would support transport, while evidence of new recruits might support another part of the explanation. Neither observation automatically excludes the other. Mechanisms can operate together. The strongest conclusion describes what the data distinguish and what remains possible, rather than forcing every change into a single cause.',
  'Food-web consequences also depend on rates and context. A cnidarian can remove small prey and itself be eaten by another animal. A temporary concentration could change local feeding, but the effect may weaken when currents disperse it. Drawing a predator–prey arrow states that an interaction can occur; estimating its effect requires information about abundance, feeding rates, duration, and alternative prey. This connects the body-level mechanisms in the article with a larger ecological explanation.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Separate a mechanism from the evidence for it',sources:['cnidBody','cnidJellies'],paragraphs:[
  'A mechanism describes a chain of events that could produce an observation. Evidence tests whether that chain actually explains the case being studied. Suppose small plankton disappear from water surrounding a jellyfish. Tentacle capture is one possible mechanism, but the observation alone cannot rule out settlement, sampling error, or another consumer. A stronger investigation compares otherwise similar water with and without the animal, records prey contacts, and checks how the counts change through time. Replication asks whether the pattern recurs rather than depending on one unusual animal or container.',
  'Measurements also need a definition that matches the question. Tentacle length might help describe the area an animal can encounter, while successful prey transfer to the mouth measures a later step. A count of discharged capsules measures something different again. These variables may be related without being interchangeable. A longer tentacle does not automatically prove a higher feeding rate because prey concentration, water movement, contact, and handling also matter. State which part of the proposed chain each measurement represents, and identify the steps that remain unobserved.',
  'An aquarium makes close observation possible but changes the setting. Walls redirect flow, prey may be supplied in pulses, and an animal cannot always move away as it would in the sea. Field observations include natural variability but make control more difficult. These are complementary kinds of evidence. Compare their conditions before combining their conclusions. For this course, practice drawing a short causal chain from structure to action to ecological effect, then place an observation beside each arrow. An unsupported arrow becomes a useful question for the next investigation rather than a fact to memorize.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Use the comparison to prepare a focused case study',sources:['cnidDiversity','cnidLion'],paragraphs:[
  'Return to the four scientific-style plates and separate direct observations from interpretations. A visible bell or branching stem is an observation within the limits of the drawing. A role in swimming, prey capture, or colony growth is an interpretation supported by biological knowledge. The plates are educational illustrations, not measurements of preserved material, and they are not displayed at one scale. A larger drawing does not establish a larger animal.',
  'Build an explanation that crosses levels in a controlled order: identify the body form, locate the relevant tissues or cells, describe their action, and connect that action with feeding or movement. Then ask what environmental condition might change the outcome. The same reasoning can compare animals without pretending that every cnidarian shares the same habitat or life history. A difference should become a question about function rather than a reason to discard a shared relationship.',
  'The next unit applies these ideas to anemones. Later in this chapter we return to them through the lion’s mane jellyfish, one of the largest jellyfish. Size comparisons need care: bell diameter and extended tentacle length describe different dimensions, while a coral colony can be larger than an individual polyp. Use the group overview as a foundation for those focused studies, carrying the distinction between a cell, an individual, a stage, and a colony into each comparison.'
 ]},
 ]},
 anemones:{title:'Anemones: soft-body mechanics, partnerships, and life on a surface',deck:'Explain the functions of a polyp using mechanisms and evidence rather than a flowerlike silhouette.',abstract:cnidarianGuides.anemones.advancedAbstract,terms:[...anemTerms,{word:'Hydrostatic support',definition:'Support and movement produced by muscles acting against contained fluid.'},{word:'Pedal laceration',definition:'Asexual production of new anemones from tissue left behind at the base in some species.'}],question:'Design repeated photographic observations that distinguish tissue growth, temporary expansion, and the arrival of new anemones. Define the scale, time interval, and individual identities.',sections:[
 {heading:'Start with an animal organized as a polyp',sources:['cnidBody'],paragraphs:[
  'Sea anemones are cnidarians in the anthozoan lineage. Their familiar adult bodies are polyps, with an oral region, tentacles, a column, and an attachment region. They do not alternate into a swimming jellyfish medusa. This does not mean that all stages remain attached or that every species has an identical reproductive cycle. The classification describes biological relationships, while the description of the adult form identifies structures whose functions can be investigated.',
  'A flower comparison captures the arrangement of a crown but can mislead about nutrition and structure. Tentacles are living animal appendages, not petals, and the mouth leads to a digestive space. The pedal disc attaches rather than acting as a plant root. Assigning a familiar land-based name to a shape is not a substitute for explaining the materials and processes involved. Begin with the actual animal structures, then use an analogy only for the limited feature it helps describe.',
  'The four selected species form a comparative set, not a single natural community. They differ in geography, habitat, and appearance. An illustration of several species at equal display size helps examine features but removes information about real size and location. Before making an ecological claim, consult the species information rather than assuming that all animals on one page share the same temperature, depth, or partners. The gallery is an entry point to comparison, not a reconstruction of one reef.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Fluid and muscles create a changing support system',sources:['cnidBody','anemBeadlet'],paragraphs:[
  'The soft column is supported in part by fluid within the body. Muscles acting against that fluid can change the body’s shape, producing expansion, contraction, or bending. This is hydrostatic support. A rigid skeleton is therefore not the only way to maintain a feeding posture. The arrangement also means that apparent width and height can change substantially without the amount of living tissue changing to the same degree.',
  'Imagine photographing one anemone before and after its tentacles withdraw. Its outline may become much smaller, but it would be wrong to calculate that difference as a loss of tissue growth. The photograph records posture and expansion as well as size. A better growth study would standardize observations as far as possible and follow repeated measurements over a suitable interval, while acknowledging that a living soft body does not hold a perfectly fixed shape.',
  'Environmental context adds another complication. Water motion can bend the tentacle crown or column, and the viewing angle can alter projected area. Comparing two photographs without recording these conditions can confound posture with growth. A scale marker solves only the magnification problem; it does not remove the effects of contraction or orientation. The method must identify which biological quantity is wanted and explain how the measurement relates to that quantity.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Attachment and movement involve different time scales',sources:['anemBeadlet','anemPlumose'],paragraphs:[
  'The pedal disc provides contact with a surface, helping many anemones remain in a useful position. That contact is a biological attachment, not simply the animal’s weight pressing on the rock. Some species can move slowly using the base, and some can detach under particular circumstances. An animal can therefore be associated with a fixed surface for feeding while still having ways to change position. The word attached should not be confused with an absolute inability to move.',
  'Consider a small area photographed once a week. An anemone missing from its original spot might have moved, died, contracted until difficult to see, or been obscured. A new individual might represent settlement, asexual reproduction, or movement into view. A count alone cannot decide among these explanations. Mapping recognizable individuals, expanding the surveyed area, and recording more frequent observations can help test whether movement rather than mortality explains the apparent loss.',
  'Flow may create both opportunities and constraints for an attached feeder. Moving water brings potential food, but the animal must retain a useful position and handle particles or prey within reach. It is not enough to say more flow is always better. Compare the actual capture process and the conditions in which the tentacles are extended or withdrawn. A proposed relationship between water speed and feeding requires observations across a defined range, not a single dramatic photograph.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Prey capture is a sequence, not just a sting',sources:['cnidBody','anemAggregating'],paragraphs:[
  'Tentacles bring cnidocytes into contact with possible prey. Capsules inside these cells can discharge threads that contribute to attachment or penetration, depending on their type. The tentacle then helps transfer suitable material toward the mouth. Several stages separate encounter from ingestion. A successful discharge is not proof that food reached the digestive cavity, and a food item touching the tentacle is not proof that it will be retained or swallowed.',
  'This distinction suggests useful response variables. An observer could record encounter rate, retention after contact, transfer to the mouth, and eventual ingestion. Each rate refers to a different part of feeding. If one condition increases contacts but decreases retention, the final food supply might not improve. Following the sequence prevents a vivid microscopic mechanism from becoming an unsupported claim about the animal’s overall nutritional success.',
  'A classroom can analyze recorded observations or a supplied sequence rather than disturbing live animals. Define an encounter before counting it, distinguish a rejected object from food that disappears behind a tentacle, and note when the camera loses sight of the mouth. These uncertainties belong in the interpretation. Transparent recording rules make the result more useful than a confident count based on assumptions about events that were not actually visible.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Digestion and exchange fit the body arrangement',sources:['cnidBody','cnidTissues'],paragraphs:[
  'The gastrovascular cavity is an internal digestive space rather than a long tube with separate entry and exit openings. Food passes through the mouth, digestion proceeds through several processes, and undigested remains can leave through the same opening. Internal partitions and tissues increase the functional complexity beyond what the external column reveals. A simple outline in a plate should not be mistaken for an empty bag with no specialized structure.',
  'Thin tissues and surrounding water also matter for the movement of dissolved gases and other materials. Muscular activity, digestion, and maintenance create demands that must be met through exchange and circulation of internal materials. These demands depend on conditions. A larger visible crown does not automatically demonstrate greater metabolism, and a period of contraction does not mean that every internal process has stopped. Body position is one observation among several, rather than a complete physiological measurement.',
  'To reason about a possible constraint, imagine the distance a dissolved substance must cross to reach a tissue. A change in tissue arrangement or water movement could affect that pathway. This thought experiment identifies a mechanism worth investigating; it does not provide a measured oxygen-use rate for a named species. Strong biological explanations separate the physical principle from the numerical claim and specify what additional measurement would be needed to connect them.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Symbiotic partners add benefits with conditions',sources:['cnidCorals','anemAggregating'],paragraphs:[
  'Some anemones host photosynthetic partners that can contribute organic products to the host. The anemone also captures animal food, and the relative contributions of those pathways depend on the species and environment. It is misleading to describe every anemone as an animal powered entirely by sunlight. A pale deep-water species and a symbiotic shallow-water species should not be assigned the same energy budget simply because both have tentacles around a mouth.',
  'A useful model separates light capture, photosynthesis, transfer of products, prey capture, and the host’s expenditure. A change in one step can affect the others, but the direction and size of the outcome need evidence. Temperature and light can change both the performance of the partners and their costs. A higher light level therefore does not automatically imply a healthier animal or a permanently stronger partnership.',
  'Some anemones associate with fishes or other animals as well. These relationships are specific interactions, not a universal invitation for any fish to enter any tentacle crown. To explain a benefit, identify what each partner gains and what costs or risks remain. Observing two species together establishes an association; demonstrating that both benefit requires evidence about outcomes such as food, shelter, survival, or reproduction under comparable conditions.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Reproduction changes the meaning of a dense patch',sources:['anemAggregating','anemPlumose'],paragraphs:[
  'Sexual reproduction combines contributions from eggs and sperm, while asexual processes can produce new individuals from an existing animal. Depending on the species, division or tissue left at the base can contribute to multiplication. New animals produced asexually may be genetically very similar, yet they remain distinct individuals with their own positions and experiences. A dense group should not automatically be interpreted as one large connected body.',
  'If a patch grows from ten visible individuals to twenty, several mechanisms could contribute: asexual production, settlement after sexual reproduction, movement, or improved detection of previously hidden animals. The number alone cannot identify the process. Marked maps, repeated observations, and information about the species’ reproductive biology help distinguish these possibilities. Any marks or methods used on living animals would need appropriate scientific oversight; existing photographs and datasets can support classroom reasoning without handling the animals.',
  'The distinction also matters when comparing populations. A large patch dominated by descendants of a few animals may differ genetically from a similarly sized patch with many independently recruited individuals. That possibility does not tell you the diversity of a real patch without data. Genetic similarity, abundance, and spatial arrangement are different properties. An ecological explanation becomes clearer when it states which property was measured instead of using the word population as though it described all three.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Competition and disturbance shape a shared surface',sources:['anemBeadlet','anemAggregating'],paragraphs:[
  'A surface provides limited space for attachment, while neighboring animals may also compete for food or affect one another through contact. Some anemones use specialized structures during interactions with neighbors. The presence of stinging cells does not mean that every encounter has an identical outcome. Size, identity, position, and environmental conditions can change how an interaction unfolds. Describe the observed contact and response rather than attributing every boundary in a patch to one cause.',
  'A bare area between neighboring groups could reflect interactions, unsuitable substrate, recent disturbance, or the history of settlement. To test a competition hypothesis, compare repeated boundary observations with nearby areas and document changes in the animals and surface. A single empty strip is suggestive, not conclusive. The investigation should identify an alternative explanation and collect evidence capable of separating it from the proposed mechanism.',
  'Human disturbance can also change the habitat through physical damage, pollution, warming, or alterations to the surrounding community. These are broad pathways rather than a prediction that every species responds the same way. Effective interpretation connects a specific change with an observed response over a defined period. The same discipline used for a feeding mechanism applies at the habitat scale: identify the sequence, consider competing explanations, and report the limits of the observations.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Compare habitats without assuming one ideal anemone',sources:['anemBeadlet','anemPlumose','anemAggregating'],paragraphs:[
  'Different species make different combinations of traits useful. A sheltered crevice, an exposed rock, and a shaded underwater surface offer different flows, light levels, and opportunities for prey contact. An individual’s expanded outline is therefore only part of its relationship with a habitat. Compare the attachment surface, the conditions when the animal was observed, and the species involved. A dense crown of tentacles is a feeding structure; it does not by itself reveal whether the animal contains photosynthetic partners or which environmental change would benefit it.',
  'A useful comparison keeps the question narrow. Instead of asking which species is best adapted, ask how one feature might help in one stated setting and what cost might accompany it. More exposed tentacles could increase encounters while also increasing exposure to disturbance. Use observations to test that possibility, and avoid treating an attractive illustration as proof of an ecological advantage.'
 ]},
 {heading:'Use the plate as evidence for comparison, not a measurement',sources:['anemBeadlet','anemPlumose','anemBubble'],paragraphs:[
  'The bubble-tip, beadlet, plumose, and aggregating anemones differ visibly in their crowns and columns. Those differences suggest useful questions about feeding surfaces, contraction, and habitat, but the illustrations do not measure rates or show every feature needed for identification. Some traits vary with posture or conditions. Compare the image with the accompanying species information and preserve the distinction between an illustrated feature and a claim about an entire species.',
  'Scientific names help identify which organism is being discussed, yet a name alone does not replace evidence. Different species can have similar outlines, and one species can vary in appearance. A responsible observation records the identification basis and uncertainty. The gallery is deliberately drawn without a shared scale; the numbers connect pictures with descriptions rather than ranking size. To compare actual size, seek measurements taken in a defined state with a reliable scale.',
  'Finish with a structure–function explanation that names the relevant part, describes its action, and connects that action with a benefit or constraint. Then state what evidence would test the explanation. For example, a broad crown might increase opportunities to encounter food, but direct observation must establish encounter and retention under the relevant flow. This approach turns an attractive illustration into the start of a biological investigation rather than the end of one.'
 ]},
 ]},
};
